  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 PM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nLarge scale upper level troughing remained in place over the \neastern CONUS early this afternoon, with an upper level ridge \naxis just east of the Rockies, leaving the forecast area under \nnorthwest flow aloft. At the surface, high pressure was centered\nover southwestern Ontario with ridging extending south into the\nforecast area. This was leading to quiet and cool weather with \nplenty of sunshine. Temperatures as of 1 PM were in the lower \nto mid 40s. Expect another widespread hard freeze tonight with \na few pieces of guidance even hinting at some patchy fog \ndevelopment near and east of the Missouri River, where surface \nwinds will remain lighter and incoming high clouds won't quite \nreach. However, model soundings show winds aloft may stay up \njust enough to preclude development. If we do end up with any \nfog, can't rule out some slick spots.\n\nThe ridge axis to our west will continue to push east through \nthe weekend combining with southerly low level flow on the back \nside of the surface high to bring us a very slow but steady \nwarming trend with high temperatures rising 2-3 degrees each \nday. We'll also stay dry through the weekend. While a shortwave \ntrough currently just off the CA coast will slide east of the \nRockies Saturday evening into Sunday, guidance is in very good \nagreement that associated precip will remain just to our \nsouthwest.\n\nThe warming trend will continue into next week as upper level \nridging amplifies over the central CONUS and southerly low level\nflow continues to strengthen. Expect widespread 50s by Tuesday \nwith a few spots making a run at 60 by Wednesday. While \ntemperatures will be pretty comfortable, it will also be pretty \nbreezy, with EPS guidance showing 30-40+ mph gusts will be \ncommon on at least Tuesday and Wednesday. The winds will also \nbring a little bit of moisture ahead of a cold front progged to \nslide through sometime Wednesday into Thursday (still a decent \namount of spread in timing in various ensembles). This will \nbring our next chances for precipitation, though guidance does \nseem to be trending toward slightly drier solutions with the \nbest moisture only clipping eastern/southeastern portions of the\nforecast area. For now, chances remain in the 20-30% range.\n\nMeanwhile, guidance is in good agreement that a trough will dig\ninto the western CONUS Thursday with a cutoff low spinning up \nand ejecting toward the area sometime Friday through next \nweekend. While this will likely bring widespread precipitation \nchances to the area, there remains a lot of spread on timing and\ntrack of the low's ejection with solutions ranging from precip \narriving early Friday to sometime on Sunday. And while rain is \nvery much favored for eastern NE and western IA, there are a few\nsolutions that bring the first snowflakes of the season to at \nleast parts of the area with the low tracking farther south \nand/or more cold air wrapping in on the back side prior to \nprecip exiting. For now, we'll give that a 10% chance of \nhappening and see how things trend over the next several days.",
